summary:
The page covers the third deployment target, **deploying a model in the browser**. Deep learning is common in browser-based and desktop JavaScript applications. Such apps *can* call a remote model over REST, but running the model directly in the browser on the user's computer (using the GPU if one is available) has key advantages. The authors recommend it when:

- you want to **offload compute** to the end user, which can dramatically cut server costs;
- the **input data must stay** on the user's computer or phone, as with the web and desktop versions of the spam-detection chat app, a cross-platform JavaScript app;
- you have **strict latency** requirements: the local model may be slower than a big server GPU, but there is no extra ~100 ms network round trip;
- the app must **keep working offline** once the model has been downloaded and cached.

There are two caveats. The model must be small enough not to *hog* the user's CPU, GPU or RAM. And because the entire model is downloaded, **nothing about it can be confidential**. The authors also warn that a trained model usually lets someone recover *some information about its training data*, so a model trained on sensitive data should not be made public.

On tooling, the TensorFlow ecosystem has **TensorFlow.js**, and ONNX has a native JavaScript runtime. TensorFlow.js implements almost all of the Keras API (its working name was *WebKeras*) plus many lower-level TensorFlow APIs, and a saved Keras model can be imported into it for use in a browser app or an Electron desktop app.

The next subsection, **Inference model optimization**, argues that optimization matters most under tight power and memory budgets (phones, embedded devices) or low-latency requirements, and should always happen *before* importing into TensorFlow.js or exporting to TensorFlow Lite. The first of two techniques, **weight pruning**, begins at the bottom of the page. Not every coefficient in a weight tensor contributes equally, so keeping only the most significant ones considerably reduces the parameter count. The text continues on p.212, which also introduces weight quantization.
